faq:
  - q: "What are the dominant industries in the New York job market?"
    a: "New York City has the most diverse and competitive professional job market in the United States. Dominant industries: (1) Financial Services — Wall Street is still the global center of investment banking, private equity, hedge funds, asset management, and financial technology. Goldman Sachs, JPMorgan, Morgan Stanley, Blackstone, Apollo, KKR, Citadel, and thousands of hedge funds and PE funds are headquartered here. (2) Technology — NYC is the second-largest tech market in the US, behind only the Bay Area. Google's Chelsea and Hudson Yards offices, Meta, Amazon, and thousands of fintech and media-tech companies operate in NYC. The technology sector in NYC has the advantage of proximity to financial services clients and capital. (3) Media, Advertising, and Publishing — NBCUniversal, News Corp, Condé Nast, Hearst, WPP, Publicis, and the global advertising industry are headquartered in New York. (4) Management Consulting — every major global consulting firm has a significant NYC presence. MBB, Big Four strategy practices, and boutique consulting firms are all active in NYC. (5) Healthcare and Life Sciences — NYU Langone, Mount Sinai, Memorial Sloan Kettering, and a growing biotech ecosystem in Midtown South."
  - q: "How is job searching in New York City different from other markets?"
    a: "New York job searching differs from other US markets in several ways: (1) Competition density — NYC concentrates the most ambitious professionals in the US into one job market. The competition for finance, consulting, and tech roles is more intense per role than in any other city. (2) Recruiter culture — Wall Street firms have structured, recruiter-driven processes that are different from tech's self-service application culture. Understanding which roles are recruiter-placed and which are applied to directly matters significantly. (3) Networking is in-person — NYC has some of the most active professional networking scenes in the US. Alumni association events, industry conferences (FinTech Week NY, Advertising Week, TechCrunch Disrupt), and dinner-meeting culture are more active in NYC than other markets. (4) State and city taxes — New York State has a marginal rate up to 10.9% and New York City adds up to 3.88% in city income tax. A $200K role in NYC carries state+city tax burden of roughly 14.78% above federal taxes. This materially affects after-tax income and should factor into offer comparisons."
  - q: "What do finance professionals need to know about the NYC job market?"
    a: "New York is the global center of buy-side and sell-side finance, and the finance job market operates on different mechanics than other industries: (1) The recruiting calendar is structured — investment banking on-cycle PE recruiting, MBA associate recruiting, and hedge fund analyst/associate searches all run on specific annual timelines that practitioners know and outsiders often miss. (2) Headhunters are gatekeepers for senior finance roles — Amity Search, GoBuyside, SHK, CPI, and others control access to the most coveted PE and HF roles. Knowing how to develop and leverage headhunter relationships is a skill that separates successful finance career moves from unsuccessful ones. (3) Networking is relationship-intensive — finance hiring in NYC is almost entirely relationship-driven above the junior analyst level. Alumni networks at Goldman, BlackRock, and KKR are powerful and actively maintained. (4) Compensation benchmarking is specific — NYC finance compensation varies significantly by firm type (bank vs. PE vs. HF), strategy (fundamental vs. quant), and year class. Generic salary data is insufficient for accurately benchmarking a NYC finance offer."
  - q: "How is NYC tech different from San Francisco tech?"
    a: "NYC's technology market has several differences from the Bay Area: (1) Finance and media adjacency — NYC tech companies frequently serve financial services and media clients; engineers and PMs with domain knowledge of these sectors have a significant advantage in NYC tech hiring over generalists. (2) Fintech concentration — NYC has the highest concentration of fintech companies in the US. Stripe, Brex, Plaid, Robinhood's NYC office, Bloomberg, and dozens of institutional fintech companies operate here. Fintech knowledge is a valuable differentiator in NYC tech job searching. (3) Compensation is slightly below Bay Area — NYC tech compensation is typically 5–15% below comparable Bay Area roles before adjusting for state+city income tax, which narrows the gap further. (4) Return-to-office pressure is higher — NYC tech companies were early to require in-office presence relative to West Coast tech companies. Full-remote arrangements are less common in NYC than in Austin or Seattle."
  - q: "What does career coaching in New York City specifically involve?"
    a: "Effective career coaching for NYC professionals addresses the city's specific dynamics: (1) Industry-specific interview preparation — finance technical prep (LBO, DCF, case studies) for Wall Street roles; product and engineering prep for NYC tech; portfolio and brand thinking for media and advertising roles. (2) NYC networking strategy — which professional associations, alumni networks, and industry events produce the highest-quality contacts in your target function. (3) New York State and City compensation negotiation — understanding after-tax comparisons, negotiating total packages that include city-specific considerations like commuter benefits and housing allowances. (4) Wall Street recruiter navigation — for finance professionals, developing headhunter relationships and understanding when to use them vs. direct outreach. (5) Career pivots within NYC — moving between finance and fintech, from media to tech, or from consulting to industry — all have specific NYC-market dynamics that affect strategy."
  - q: "What are the best networking strategies for NYC job seekers?"
    a: "New York City has a rich professional networking ecosystem: (1) Alumni associations — NYC alumni associations for elite universities (Harvard Club, Columbia Club, etc.) host regular industry-specific events and are active referral networks. (2) Industry associations — Council on Foreign Relations (policy/international), Financial Women's Association, 100 Women in Finance, Urban Land Institute, and dozens of industry-specific professional organizations run active programming. (3) LinkedIn outreach works in NYC but in-person follow-up is expected — NYC professionals respond well to LinkedIn connection requests that lead to coffee meetings or brief in-person conversations. Cold LinkedIn messages that don't result in any meeting are rarely productive. (4) Conference attendance — advertising and media professionals network at Advertising Week NY, Cannes Lions NY, and related events. Finance professionals use events like the Milken Institute Global Conference and SkyBridge Capital SALT Conference. Tech professionals use NY Tech Week and events hosted at WeWork and similar co-working communities. (5) NYC neighborhood events — industry clusters tend to congregate geographically. Finance in Midtown/Downtown, tech in Midtown South and Hudson Square, media in Midtown and the West Side. Industry-specific happy hours and meetups in these areas create high-density networking opportunities."
