      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Finance job searching in New York City</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Know the recruiting calendar cold.</strong> On-cycle PE recruiting, MBA associate recruiting, and analyst-level banking searches all run on specific, firm-level timelines. Missing the window by 2–3 weeks is often permanent for that cycle.</li>
        <li><strong>Build headhunter relationships early.</strong> For PE and HF moves above the junior level, retained search firms are the primary sourcing channel. Develop relationships with Amity, GoBuyside, SHK, and CPI before you need them — not when you're actively searching.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical preparation is non-negotiable.</strong> NYC finance interviews are among the most technically demanding in the market. LBO modeling, DCF, and accounting knowledge must be fluent, not familiar. Investment idea presentations (for HF roles) must be polished and convictional.</li>
        <li><strong>Compensation includes significant bonus variability.</strong> Wall Street compensation is heavily back-end loaded — base salary is a fraction of total comp for senior finance professionals. Understand the bonus history, payout structure, and timing at any firm you're considering.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Tech and media job searching in New York</h2>
      <h3>NYC Technology</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Finance and media domain knowledge is a differentiator in NYC tech — engineers and PMs who understand the client industries are more competitive than generalists at comparable companies</li>
        <li>Fintech roles require regulatory awareness (SEC, FINRA, CFTC for capital markets fintech; CFPB for consumer fintech) — demonstrate this knowledge explicitly in interviews</li>
        <li>NYC tech networking: NY Tech Week, Meetup.com tech events, and Hacker News NY community are active channels</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Media, Advertising, and Publishing</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Entertainment and media roles are networking-intensive — personal connections through alumni networks, industry associations, and social events drive most senior hiring</li>
        <li>Advertising and PR roles: portfolio and campaign results are evaluated, not just behavioral interviews — prepare specific, quantified campaign performance examples</li>
        <li>Digital media and content strategy roles sit at the intersection of media and tech — LinkedIn, content creation, and audience-building are relevant credentials</li>
